<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guadalupe Victoria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1786">1786</a>–<a href="/years/1843">1843</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as José Miguel Fernández y Félix</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guadalupe Victoria, born José Miguel Fernández y Félix, was a general of the Mexican War of Independence and the first President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, from 1824 to 1829. He took his adopted name in honour of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/our-lady-of-guadalupe">Virgin of Guadalupe</a> and of victory. His government won recognition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and began the expulsion of Spanish residents, and he was the only president to complete his term for decades.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guadalupe-victoria.jpg" alt="Guadalupe Victoria" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlos Paris · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guadalupe_Victoria_-_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Guadalupe Victoria was born on 29 September 1786 at Tamazula, in the province of Nueva Vizcaya in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, now the state of Durango. His parents died early in his childhood and he lived with his uncle, the parish priest. In 1807 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, entered the College of San Ildefonso and graduated as a Bachelor of Laws on 24 April 1811.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1812 he joined the insurgents and fought beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-morelos">José María Morelos</a> at the siege of Cuautla. For his part in the assault on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a> on 25 November 1812 he was given command of the insurgent army in Veracruz, and he took the name Guadalupe, for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/our-lady-of-guadalupe">Virgin of Guadalupe</a>, and Victoria, for the victory. Using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics, he held the Puente del Rey, the crossing between Xalapa and the port of Veracruz, and attacked royalist convoys until 1815, when he was defeated.</p><p>He then spent almost four years hiding in the jungle, while most insurgents accepted a viceregal pardon and only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicente-guerrero">Vicente Guerrero</a> kept fighting. He reappeared on 30 December 1820 and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-iguala">Plan of Iguala</a> in 1821. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a> became emperor, Victoria wanted a republic and was jailed in 1822, but escaped. He joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Antonio López de Santa Anna</a>'s revolt in December 1822, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-casa-mata">Plan of Casa Mata</a> forced Iturbide to abdicate on 19 March 1823. On 31 March Congress made Victoria, Nicolás Bravo and Pedro Celestino Negrete the Supreme Executive Power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Seventeen states voted for Victoria in the presidential election of 1824, and on 2 October 1824 he was declared the first president of the United Mexican States. His constitutional term began on 1 April 1825. He had to rebuild an economy devastated by the war and the Spanish embargo. Spending averaged about 18 million pesos a year against half that in revenue, and two British loans, each of over 3 million pounds, averted bankruptcy.</p><p>He won recognition from the United Kingdom, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a> and Gran Colombia, and ratified the border with the United States under the Adams-Onís Treaty. In 1829 he handed the presidency to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicente-guerrero">Vicente Guerrero</a>, and he was the only president to complete his term in more than 30 years of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. He later served as senator, governor of Puebla and president of the Senate, and died on 21 March 1843 at the fortress of Perote, where he was being treated for epilepsy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guadalupe Victoria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It created the United Mexican States, with nineteen states and four territories, and <b>Guadalupe Victoria</b>, a hero of the independence war, became the first president.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-mexican-republic">First Mexican Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tamazula de Victoria, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in San Carlos Fortress, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.97,-106.97&amp;year=1823" aria-label="Tamazula de Victoria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Guadalupe Victoria"><image href="/maps/globes/g240n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6002 5372h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6002 5372h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.97,-106.97&amp;year=1823">What was here in 1823? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
